    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d552">

                <pb facs="0696" n="584" sameAs="frus:frus1918/pg584"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 711.12/106</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Fletcher</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1135</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-06-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 19, 1918</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my despatch No. 1123,
                    of June 12, 1918,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d552-f1"/> I have the honor to forward
                    herewith summaries and translations of the articles which have appeared in the
                    press of Mexico City in the past week with reference to President Wilson’s
                    speech to the Mexican newspaper men.</p>
                <p>Two days after President Wilson’s speech was published here, the Mexican
                    Government, in an endeavor to prove its insincerity and to destroy the good
                    effect which was undoubtedly being made, furnished through Aguirre Berlanga, the
                    Minister of the Interior, all the newspapers of Mexico City with a translation
                    of my note of April 2,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d552-f2"/> protesting against the
                    petroleum decree of February 19, and ordered them to print it with display
                    headings. The Department will see by the clippings herewith that these
                    instructions were carried out. The <hi rendition="#italic">Democrata</hi>
                    (German) and the <hi rendition="#italic">Pueblo</hi> (Government) gave the note
                    a scare head as a threat of intervention on the part of the United States.</p>
                <p>I invite particular attention to the editorial in the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Pueblo</hi> of the 14th (a summary of which was telegraphed to the
                    Department in my telegram No. 1175, of June 14, 1 p.m.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d552-f3"/>) The article faithfully reflects the attitude of
                    President Carranza and his Government. I have been told that it was written by
                    the Minister of the Interior himself. Be this as it may—the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Pueblo</hi> is the mouthpiece of the Government and the
                    article was inspired by President Carranza.</p>
                <pb facs="0697" n="585" xml:id="pg585"/>
                <p>Under orders of the Government, apparently, the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Universal</hi> and the <hi rendition="#italic">Pueblo</hi> have been
                    running a series of editorials attacking my note. The <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Pueblo</hi> carried all its editorials under the heading, “President Wilson
                    Threatens Mexico.”</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Henry P. Fletcher</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>




